# A member lookup through a qualifier that supplies nothing used to be compiled
# as the BARE NAME of the member, and the diagnostic then said
# `undefined variable (Foo)` — naming the attribute and never the qualifier
# that actually failed, so the message pointed at the wrong half of the line.
# It was the first wall on four html5lib files, where it read as a typo.
#
# `zlib` is a Pascal RTL unit wearing a Python module's name, and it is on the
# curated bare-import list in pasparser_proc.inc — which is what makes it
# resolve to the unit rather than to a host C header.
#
# THIS FIXTURE USED `strings` AND THAT WAS WRONG FROM THE START. Its comment
# claimed `strings` was "a Pascal RTL unit wearing a Python module's name"; it
# is not reached that way at all. `strings` is DELIBERATELY absent from that
# list (an ordinary Pascal unit that happens to share a Python name), so the
# bare import falls through to /usr/include/strings.h, synthesises
# `libstrings.so` from the header's own file name, and produced a binary with a
# DT_NEEDED no loader can satisfy — verified against the PINNED compiler, whose
# build of `import strings` links and then dies at exec with
# `libstrings.so: cannot open shared object file`. The test never ran its
# binary, so it never saw that. Six of the seven RTL/header name collisions on
# this box (math, menu, netdb, png, regex, zlib) resolve to the unit; strings
# was the one that did not.
# bug-n-an-attribute-on-an-unresolved-import-degrades-to-a-bare-name
#
# The companion test test_nilpy_bare_name_error_stays_short is the control: a
# genuinely bare undefined name must KEEP the short message, because there is
# no receiver to name and inventing one would be the same defect pointing the
# other way.

import zlib
print(zlib.Foo)
